{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "For truly throwaway exploration, speed matters more than code quality, so accept AI output and judge it only by whether it demonstrates the idea. Keep prototypes isolated from real users, credentials, and production data, and delete them rather than promoting them."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo carries reputational risk and often becomes the seed of an MVP, so review demo-critical paths selectively and test the end-to-end flow. Do not let vibe-coded glue handle real credentials or sensitive data."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real early users mean real consequences, so AI can write most code but changes need selective review plus automated tests and behavioral checks. Raise risky areas such as auth, payments, permissions, and data handling to level 2 with line-by-line review."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and an on-call team require every change to be understood and owned, so a person should review all AI-written code line by line before merge. Tests, CI, and monitoring are backstops, not substitutes for review."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 0,
      "rationale": "For safety-critical or regulated production code, AI-written code creates an unacceptable verification and traceability burden, so humans should author it under the applicable standards. AI may assist with non-production artifacts such as tests, documentation, and analysis, subject to the same controls."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Use AI aggressively where mistakes are cheap and contained, and taper toward human authorship as real users, money, safety, and regulatory exposure enter the picture. The level should follow blast radius rather than enthusiasm: 4 for throwaway prototypes, 3 for demos and early MVPs, 2 for production, and 0 for critical code. At every level, AI-generated code must still go through the team's secrets management, testing, and release accountability."
}

